@prefix water: <http://example.org/orexis/water#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/water#> .
@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix market: <http://example.org/orexis/market#> .
@prefix sim:  <http://example.org/orexis/sim#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/water> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis water domain" ;
    rdfs:comment """The v1 domain, a PLUG-IN. Everything above this file is domain-neutral: agents, capabilities, polling, markets, actuation. This module says what is being sensed and allocated here and what a bid MEANS in this domain. Water is what is ALLOCATED; what is sensed is wider and grew as the bench did — soil moisture, air temperature, air humidity, illuminance. A property earns its place here by being something a plant cares about, not by being water. Swap this file and the society still runs.""" .

#################  The things  #################

:Plant a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf sosa:FeatureOfInterest ; rdfs:label "Plant" ;
    rdfs:comment "The physical thing sensed and watered. It has no stake — its agent does." .
:WaterSource a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Water source" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a supplier dispenses; the plumbing node a market forms around." .
:SoilMoisture a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Soil moisture" .

#  THE GOOD (#198): what this domain's lots are quantities of. One individual, because one
#  thing flows through every pipe here — the barrel's venue and the city's venue sell the SAME
#  water against different properties, which is exactly why the venue tie routes through this
#  node instead of pinning a property on either.
:Water a market:Good ; rdfs:label "Water" .

#  CONSERVATION, stated once: every litre a valve dispenses left some source, so a water
#  source's level moves -1.0 per dispensed litre — true of the CLASS, by physics, which is
#  what an owl:hasValue restriction is for (the DHT's datasheet pattern; the closure entails
#  it onto every barrel, and no model node restates it).
#  Beside it, two more facts true of every water source by what it IS, the same pattern twice:
#  it VENDS water ('the lot states its good' — the venue derivation reads the entailed triple,
#  so no world restates the obvious), and receiving is conservation's mirror — a litre poured
#  in is a litre held, so the litres-per-stored-litre conversion is identically 1.0 and stating
#  it per barrel would be the #164 copy again.
:WaterSource rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sim:doseEffect ; owl:hasValue -1.0 ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty market:supplies ; owl:hasValue :Water ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty :litresPerStoredLitre ; owl:hasValue 1.0 ] .

:StoredLitres a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Stored litres" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much water a source is actually holding — the stock behind every promise the market makes. Observable because a float or level sensor can witness it, and observed so that an empty barrel is a fact an agent can hold rather than a surprise the pots discover. The first property on the SUPPLY side of the wire: a dispensed litre lowers it, which is the sign convention the stand-ins carry." .
:AirTemperature a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Air temperature" ;
    rdfs:comment "Sensed about a plant, and allocated to nobody — no market relieves it, so no agent bids on it. It is here because a second property of the same pot is what makes an observation's key matter: the temp/humidity board on the bench observes this, and the water market must not read it as dryness." .
:Illuminance a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Illuminance" ;
    rdfs:comment """How much light falls where a plant is standing. Here for the same reason air temperature is — a plant cares about it, this domain is what a plant cares about — and NOT in the species package that first needed it, because light is nobody's species in particular. A package owns its namespace and may not name a term that is not its own; `zz:Illuminance` would have made every other plant borrow from the Zamioculcas.

Nothing measures it. That is the honest state and not a reason to leave it unsaid: a range for a property no instrument reports is a fact about the plant that becomes checkable the day someone adds a light sensor, and until then it is a claim in the graph rather than a sentence in a comment. Note the asymmetry with soil moisture, which no market relieves either but which every agent has a desire in — this one has neither yet.

Denominated in lux, and stated as such with `unit:LUX`, because unlike a moisture fraction this is a real quantity with a real unit that horticultural sources actually use.""" .

:AirHumidity a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Air humidity" ;
    rdfs:comment "The other half of that board, and the more dangerous half: it is a FRACTION, so its readings land in the same 0-1 range as soil moisture and inside the bands agents hold. A temperature mistaken for moisture reads as absurdly wet and is merely ignored; a humidity mistaken for moisture reads as a plausible, actionable dryness and gets bid on. Nothing in the number says which it is — only the property does." .

:AirPressure a sosa:ObservableProperty ; rdfs:label "Air pressure" ;
    rdfs:comment "Weather, not the plant: no range a species states mentions it and no market relieves it. It is here for the same structural reason Illuminance is — a property must be named before a channel can observe it — and it earned the naming the day the terrace deployment wanted its BME280's third value on a graph rather than only on the wire. Denominated in hectopascals (unit:HectoPA), the unit the part's own ontology already states its range in. The alternative, a weather domain package of one term, is deferred until something besides a dashboard reads it." .

:servedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Plant ; rdfs:range :WaterSource ;
    rdfs:label "served by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Plumbing/reachability: which source can water this plant." .
:suppliedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:subPropertyOf market:offeredBy ; rdfs:domain :WaterSource ; rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "supplied by" .
:capacityL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :WaterSource ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf market:lotCapacity , sim:maxValue ;
    rdfs:label "capacity litres" ;
    rdfs:comment "Physical ceiling on what this source can dispense — the constitution's total-allocation limit. A subproperty of market:lotCapacity, which is the word the KERNEL reads (#148): the world states litres, the closure entails the market's term, and the loader that hands clearing its limit names no domain. Also of sim:maxValue since the barrel learned to run dry: a level stand-in's ceiling IS the capacity, and restating 5.0 on the model would be the #164 copy all over again — one statement, both readers entailed." .

#################  Physics of a pot (public: it is how the world behaves)  #################

:driesPerDay a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Plant ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf sim:losesPerDay ;
    rdfs:label "dries per day" ;
    rdfs:comment "How fast this pot loses moisture, as a fraction of saturation per simulated day — the pot's own physics, stated publicly because it is how the world behaves (#164). Was dryRatePerTick, a figure whose tick was never defined and which nothing had ever read: the stand-ins ran on a private copy (the kernel model term, on the device model) restating the same fact in different units. The subproperty bridge is the whole mechanism, on the capacityLitres -> market:lotCapacity precedent: the world states the pot's physics in the domain's term, the closure entails the kernel's, and the simulator generation reads the conclusion naming no domain. The model term survives as an OVERRIDE for values that are not anyone's pot — a room's temperature drifts on the model's say-so, a pot dries on its own." .

# Deliberately un-domained. On a PLANT (in the world) it is the physical fact: what that pot
# actually takes. On an AGENT (in its own beliefs) it is what the agent BELIEVES its pot
# takes, which is what its bid is computed from. They coincide today; an agent that learns
# would revise its own copy, and being wrong about it would cost it money.
:litresPerFraction a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "litres per fraction" ;
    market:ofGood :Water ;
    market:aboutProperty :SoilMoisture ;
    market:direction market:Raises ;
    rdfs:comment "Litres needed to raise moisture by 1.0 — converts a deficit into a demand. What it is a fraction OF is stated here rather than left to the reader: the whole valuation (this, maxValuePerL, and the aim asked of desire) is denominated in soil moisture, and an agent holding readings of several properties must not price a humidity. This statement hung on water:hasTarget while that term existed; the denomination outlived the point. The DIRECTION is stated beside it now — water raises moisture — which reads as too obvious to write until you notice nothing else says it: the reflex steered by a hardcoded sign, and a menu of what acts DO could not be derived at all. Since #198 the GOOD is stated as the third of the sentence's three parts: a lot of water, priced in moisture, raising it — because litres-per-fraction is a claim about water in particular, and a venue selling anything else must never borrow it." .

#  The refill valuation (#198 / barrel arc 4): the same good, priced in the SUPPLY side's own
#  property. A buyer whose subject is a water source — a dealer restocking — converts its
#  StoredLitres deficit into litres of demand at exactly 1.0, which is why the VALUE reaches
#  every source entailed from the class above and only the agent's own BELIEF copy is authored
#  (the copy an agent that learned its barrel leaks would revise, and be right to).
:litresPerStoredLitre a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "litres per stored litre" ;
    market:ofGood :Water ;
    market:aboutProperty :StoredLitres ;
    market:direction market:Raises ;
    rdfs:comment "Litres of the good needed to raise a source's stored litres by one — identically 1.0, by conservation, and stated anyway because the identity is what makes the machinery uniform: the bidder, the menu, the compose generator and the shapes all follow ofGood/aboutProperty/direction off whatever valuation term applies, and the refill market's term carrying a trivial number is better than the refill market being a special case in four files. The direction reads as tautological until the drain side is beside it: a litre DISPENSED lowers StoredLitres (modelDoseEffect -1.0), a litre BOUGHT raises it, and which of the two a venue means is exactly what the venue tie now says." .

#################  What a bid means here — the bidder's private valuation  #################
#
#  `water:hasTarget` was here and is GONE, to `sensing:aims`. It fused two things: the point an
#  agent steers its moisture toward (desire's — any agent with a stake holds one, market or no
#  market) and the input a litre-deficit is priced from (this domain's). What this domain keeps
#  is the pricing: how a deficit becomes litres, what a litre is worth, and the statement of
#  WHICH property the whole valuation is denominated in — which now hangs on litresPerFraction,
#  the term that is most literally "per fraction OF something". A bidder asks whoever provides
#  the desire family for the aim in that property; it holds no point of its own.

:maxValuePerL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "max value per litre" ;
    rdfs:comment "Willingness to pay at peak urgency — the private value curve. The market sees the bid, never this." .

#################  Judgment — desire-relative, never recorded as fact  #################

:Band a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Moisture band" ;
    rdfs:comment "A verdict, not testimony. Computed by each agent from a reading against ITS OWN region, and stored nowhere: the same 0.18 is LOW for a fern and OK for a succulent." .
:LOW  a :Band ; rdfs:label "LOW" .
:OK   a :Band ; rdfs:label "OK" .
:HIGH a :Band ; rdfs:label "HIGH" .

#  `water:bandLow` and `water:bandHigh` were here and are GONE, in favour of the region
#  `packages/capability/desire/` deduces from what a plant states it needs.
#
#  They were two decimals in a private file with nothing to answer to, and the gap showed most
#  clearly in the comment the low one carried for months: *"a comfort limit, desire-relative —
#  not the rot limit, which is physical and the constitution's"*. The rot limit is the survival
#  range's ceiling, the comfort limit is the operating range's, and both are the PLANT's — so an
#  agent restating them privately, in numbers only it could see, was keeping a second copy of a
#  public fact and was free to keep a wrong one.
#
#  The three words survive above, because the verdict is still the agent's to compute and still
#  never recorded. What is gone is the idea that the agent gets to pick where the edges are.
#  See knowledge/decisions/desire-is-deduced-from-the-ranges-the-world-states.md.
